The June meeting of Washington DC Linux user group will take place
on Wednesday, June 7, 1995, at 7pm, at the Lippsett auditorium
at NIH (see directions below). 

The meeting will feature a talk 

	LINUX SERVICES FOR MACINTOSH AND WINDOWS USERS

by Rob Newberry. Rob is a Mac/Windows developer that chose Linux
to provide network services to his development PCs. He is active
in development of Windows/Mac connectivity features in the Linux
kernel. Here's the abstract of his talk:

    I will provide an overview and some detailed examples of how
    to provide Linux services to Macintosh and Windows users on
    your network.  I will breifly cover basic TCP/IP services such
    as email and news from the Macintosh/Windows user side --
    including information on what freeware/shareware clients are
    available and where to get them.  I will also provide a more
    detailed analysis of file and print services for Appletalk and
    Microsoft networks, including the Columbia Appletalk Package,
    Netatalk, and SAMBA.  I hope to have some bit of information
    about current developments in IPX and Novell connectivity.
    Finally, I will conclude my talk with a look at HylaFAX, a FAX
    server package available for Linux, which has "print-to-fax"
    clients available for both Windows and Macintosh.


