Greetings from your moderator.

As you might have noticed, articles in comp.os.linux.announce haven't
been spreading throughout the world very quickly; some, it seems, not
at all.  I have therefore reposted a bunch of them; all reposts have
a subject line beginning with ``REPOST''.  Sorry about that.

The reposts were done from Yggdrasil's machine, in the hope that this
will make fewer problems.  (It seems that the problem with the slow
article propagation was or is in Finland or close by.)  My thanks to
Adam Richter and Yggdrasil for the account.  Several other Linux-related
companies have also offered accounts (I asked for them :-), and I will
be using these to set up an automatic system for watching the propagation.
More on this later, if necessary.  (In a world of paranoids, it is probably
necessary to note that no company will receive a special treatment for
giving me an account for comp.os.linux.announce.  Nor for not giving.
I am and will remain partial on completely other grounds. :-)

Some other administrative notes from your friendly (if slow) moderator:

* The submission address has changed (quite a while ago), but I'm still
  getting submissions via the old address.  The new address is
  linux-announce@news.ornl.gov.  Please use that instead of the old
  Cornell address (which, for some curious reason, still works, but
  might stop working at whim).

* Many submissions contain very little in the way of description.  Not
  everyone knows what every piece of software does, so a description is
  very important.  A reference to some other obscure piece of software
  is not enough; neither is referring to some kind of application area
  that few have heard of.

* Please include a properly filled in LSM form, if relevant.  If you
  fill in the LSM form properly, you don't even have to write anything
  else for the announcement.  (If you include the LSM form in the
  announcement, you don't have to send it to me separately as well
  -- I'm also the LSM maintainer -- since I will rip it out of c.o.l.a
  anyway.)

* Please indicate the size of the files (in the LSM form or otherwise) and
  the exact and complete pathnames of the files.  This makes it easier 
  on people with slow or no ftp connections.  Please at least make a
  guess of the final location; it might even be nice to wait until the
  package has been moved from the upload directory.

* Please be careful when replying to articles in moderated groups or received
  via the mailing list mirror of comp.os.linux.announce.  For some
  reason or other, I'm getting many replies that were intended to the actual
  poster.  This is very annoying and it takes time to respond to each
  article.

* Please don't post questions to comp.os.linux.announce (or directly to
  me).  They just make more noise in my mailbox.  (Although, I have to admit,
  I don't always reply to these letters.)

* Please don't send requests to join the mailing list mirror of c.o.l.announce
  to the article submission address.  (I have to reply to these, otherwise
  the senders try it again and again; I've sometimes received half a dozen
  requests from the same person.)  The correct address is
  majordomo@vger.rutgers.edu .

* If you find an announcement to be inappropriate, please do send mail to
  me explaining why.

* If you post an announcement to comp.os.linux.announce, do not also post
  it separately to other Linux groups, unless you absolutely must avoid
  moderator delays.

* Please avoid cross-posts.  Cross-posts within Linux groups are deleted
  and cross-posts outside Linux groups require special checking by me.
  Cross-posting to several moderated groups means that each moderator
  will have to approve the article and this takes time.  For announcements,
  there is seldom a problem with having a few copies posted to different
  moderated groups.

* Don't send tests to me.  I will continue to ignore them.

* Please include keywords.  Otherwise I have to invent them and they
  probably won't be as good.

* Please post timing critical announcements as early as possible, and indicate
  in the _subject_ that it is urgent.  I don't always look at more than the
  subject before I put it away for later processing.  Oh, and put the date
  at the _beginning_ of the subject (my windows usually show only 80
  characters per line, not all of which is the subject).

* Keep it short and to the point.  That is easier for everyone to read
  (and helps keep moderation time short).

Enough complaints.  I hope this note will find you well (or at least
that it will find you :-).

Have a nice summer.  Or winter, if you're southwardly inclined.

-- 
Lars.Wirzenius@helsinki.fi  (finger wirzeniu@klaava.helsinki.fi)
